#Name
simple shell- shell, the standard command language interpreter. This shell is a program that acts as a user interface for communicating with an operating system. This was created to emulate how a shell operates while adding new functions to account for new parameters that would be in a traditional shell. 

#Synopsis

shell [command]

#Description
This simple shell works like a basic shell that works with the kernel of an operating system that runs commands, reads from a command line string and the standard input. When standard input is run (interactively) in terminal, hsh prints a "$$$ " prompt, reads one line, and executes it, repeating until end-of-file (Ctrl-D). When standard input is not interactive  terminal (piped or redirected) no prompt is printed which matches the output of /bin/sh.

The shell accounts for single word commands and multiple commands which is separated by a "|" symbol. This allows for each commands to be executed in sequence.It navigates through our shells environment variable by navigating PATH through the directories till it hits a file that matches that command.  If our command can't be executed through PATH, an error is printed to standard error in the form of " prog_name: command: not found" 

SIGNALS :
The shell catches SIGINT (ctrl + C). At an interactive prompt, SIGINT interrupts the current input line and re-displays the prompt and the shell is not terminated. A command running as a child process is unaffected by this handling and responds to SIGINT normally.

END OF FILE :
On reaching end of file "EOF" (ctrl + D) interactive or piped input, the shell exits with the status of the last command executed.

